# The repository is developed on Windows and deployed on Linux, and most of
# it is checked in with CRLF. That is harmless for Python, TypeScript and
# Markdown, and it is not harmless for these three.
#
# A shell script with CRLF line endings fails on Linux with `bad interpreter:
# /bin/bash^M`. The CloudFormation templates are worse than that, because the
# EC2 instance's UserData *is* a shell script embedded in YAML: a CRLF
# template boots an instance whose container never starts, and the failure
# surfaces as a health check that never goes green rather than as anything
# naming line endings.
#
# An editor or a script that rewrites one of these on Windows will introduce
# CRLF silently. This makes git refuse to store it.

*.sh        text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
Dockerfile  text eol=lf

# The web fonts are IBM's build of Plex, checked in because Google's is
# stripped of the OpenType features this interface depends on. They are binary
# and must be stored byte for byte. Git usually infers that, but this
# repository is developed on Windows and cloned by people whose `core.autocrlf`
# is not ours to set: a font that has had its CRLFs "fixed" does not fail
# loudly, it fails as a browser quietly refusing to parse it.
*.woff2     binary
*.woff      binary
*.ttf       binary
*.otf       binary
